.UPDATE...
Issued at 1103 AM PDT Wed Sep 23 2026

The OAK 12Z sounding measured the marine layer around 1400 feet deep
this morning, which is roughly 1000 feet shallower compared to
yesterday`s sounding. This morning, poor humidity recoveries around
20-30% were observed across the Diablo Range, Santa Cruz Mountains,
and the Santa Lucia range. Another round of poor humidity recoveries
is expected through tomorrow morning. A very weak push of offshore
flow will persist through the overnight hours as high pressure
continues to build over us, further compressing the marine layer
tonight into Thursday morning. While current temperatures are
running about 4-6 degrees below the forecast, they are still
expected to rise this afternoon as stratus continues to dissipate.
Consequently, pockets of moderate HeatRisk will impact the interior
East/South Bay and Central Coast valleys as well as the Big Sur
coastline into tomorrow. Come early Friday morning, a dry cold
frontal passage with a strong onshore push will allow the marine
layer to deepen and provide relief with cooler conditions through
the weekend.

